The `standalone` bundle solves a very concrete problem: you want to draw a TikZ picture, or typeset a single table, without dragging the whole preamble and page setup of your main document along. It comes in two pieces. As a **class**, `standalone` wraps your fragment into a complete document and uses the `preview` machinery to crop the PDF to the content, so the resulting file is exactly as large as the drawing (plus a border). As a **package**, it is loaded in the main document and provides `\includestandalone`, which pulls the cropped PDF in like a graphic.

```latex
% figures/circle.tex
\documentclass[tikz,border=2pt]{standalone}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \draw (0,0) circle (1);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

The `mode` key is where the real value lies. `mode=build` calls `pdflatex` on the sub-file at every compile, `mode=buildnew` only when the sub-file changed (it relies on `latexmk` semantics and therefore on `-shell-escape`), and `mode=tex` inlines the code so that `\ref` and `\cite` keep working. Because the sub-file is a proper document, you can compile it on its own and get instant feedback while iterating on a figure.

```latex
\usepackage{standalone}
...
\includestandalone[mode=buildnew]{figures/circle}
```

Two details bite newcomers. First, `standalone` crops only what is *typeset*; text that runs to a paragraph width will still fill the page unless you pass `varwidth` (or `preview=false` for full pages). Second, relative paths such as `\input` or `\includegraphics{img/foo}` inside a sub-file are resolved relative to the *main* document when you compile it — the class provides `\subfix{...}` to prefix the sub-file's own directory. Use `\ifstandalone` to guard code that only makes sense in one of the two contexts.

`subfiles` lets every chapter of a long document be a compilable LaTeX file *and* a plain `\input` of the main document. The trick is a class option: each sub-file starts with `\documentclass[../main.tex]{subfiles}`, naming the main file. When the sub-file is compiled on its own, `subfiles` reads the main file's preamble (everything before `\begin{document}`) and then typesets only the sub-file's body. When the main file is compiled, it loads `\usepackage{subfiles}` and `\subfile{chapter1}` behaves like `\input`, skipping the sub-file's own `\documentclass` block.

```latex
% main.tex
\documentclass{book}
\usepackage{subfiles}
\begin{document}
\subfile{chapters/one}
\subfile{chapters/two}
\end{document}

% chapters/one.tex
\documentclass[../main.tex]{subfiles}
\begin{document}
\chapter{One}
...
\end{document}
```

The payoff is a fast edit-compile loop: `pdflatex chapters/one` produces a PDF with the correct fonts, macros and page geometry but only one chapter, and cross-references still work after two runs. `\ifSubfilesClassLoaded{...}{...}` lets you add material (a title page, a bibliography) only when compiling standalone.

Things to watch out for: anything in a sub-file's own preamble is *ignored* when the file is pulled into the main document, so macros must live in the main preamble; `\subfile` does not issue a `\clearpage` (unlike `\include`), so add one yourself if floats from the previous chapter should be flushed; and relative paths in sub-files are resolved from the current working directory, which means `\graphicspath{{chapters/}}` or `import` is often needed for figures.

`academicons` provides font-based icons for scholarly services — Google Scholar, ResearchGate, ORCID, arXiv, HAL, Semantic Scholar, Zenodo and many more — with names such as `\aiGoogleScholar`, `\aiResearchGate`, `\aiArXiv` and `\aiOrcid`. Being a font, it inherits the current text size and colour, so an icon inside a footnote or a table cell stays proportionate.

```latex
\usepackage{academicons}
\usepackage{hyperref}
...
\href{https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=...}{\aiGoogleScholar}
```

The normal pattern is to nest the icon inside `\href` so the whole glyph is clickable; add a small `\,` or `~` if you also print the profile name. The package works with pdfLaTeX (Type1 fonts) and with XeLaTeX/LuaLaTeX, where the font is picked up automatically.

One caveat: `academicons` does not load `fontawesome5`, and the two use different glyph sets, so `\ai...` and `\fa...` names never collide but the icons may look stylistically different side by side. Pick one family for a consistent author block.

`units` is the veteran package for typesetting physical quantities: `\unit{9.81}{\metre\per\second\squared}` produces 9.81 m/s², and `\unitfrac{...}{...}` handles fractions. It also offers `\nicefrac` for vulgar fractions and lets you define units with `\usk`/`\uskdef`.

```latex
\usepackage[tight]{units}
\unit{9.81}{\metre\per\second\squared}
\unit[1]{kg}
```

The options `tight`, `medium` and `loose` control the spacing between number and unit, matching different typographic conventions; `thinspace` and `squaren` are also available. The macros `\metre`, `\second`, `\kilogram` etc. are predefined, so a document that uses the package reads quite naturally.

Having said that, `units` is effectively superseded by `siunitx`, which adds number formatting, rounding, range and list support, table alignment (`S` columns) and full locale awareness. If you are starting a new document, use `siunitx` and translate `\unit{9.81}{\metre\per\second\squared}` to `\qty{9.81}{\metre\per\second\squared}` (or the older `\SI`). `units` is still encountered in legacy templates and is fine for simple cases, but it cannot format the number, only the unit.
